data.content:
Here's the lightest approach — use the **built-in `TimeProvider`** (.NET 8+). No DI container needed; just accept it as a constructor parameter:

```csharp
// Production usage
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider.System);
```

```csharp
// In tests (add NuGet: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing)
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is lightest:**
- `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+ — no custom interface needed
- No DI container required — just pass it via the constructor
- `FakeTimeProvider` gives you full control (set time, advance, etc.)
- Parallel-safe by design — no shared mutable state

Add the testing helper to your test project:
```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```
